olvement while minimizing surgical removal. [Data: Relationships (2029)]\\n\\nThe entity description for CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY further supports this usage by noting it is used to sample suspected sites of spread, including lymph nodes, to determine whether cancer is present. Together, these records show lymph nodes as an important diagnostic decision-point in the community’s workflow. [Data: Entities (838); Relationships (2029)]\"},{\"summary\":\"WIDE NEEDLE functions as a specialized sampling tool tied to tissue sample extraction\",\"explanation\":\"WIDE NEEDLE is described as the needle used in a core needle biopsy to extract a tissue sample from a lymph node while leaving the rest of the node in place. This places it as a specialized instrument choice for nodal sampling in a tissue-preserving manner. [Data: Entities (1248)]\\n\\nIts relationship clarifies its immediate purpose: the wide needle is used to remove a tissue sample for testing. This links the instrument to the downstream diagnostic objective (laboratory/pathology evaluation) through the sampled material. [Data: Relationships (2031)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Bone marrow aspiration extends the community’s scope beyond solid-tissue cores to liquid marrow sampling\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes a distinct diagnostic collection mode: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE, with the relationship specifying that a hollow needle enters bone to enable withdrawal of liquid marrow. This indicates that the same general needle-based access concept is applied to a different anatomical compartment (bone and marrow) and a different specimen type (liquid marrow rather than tissue cores). [Data: Relationships (5657)]\\n\\nThe HOLLOW NEEDLE entity description supports this dual-use capability by stating it is used to penetrate skin and bone to collect marrow samples and can allow liquid marrow to be withdrawn for laboratory examination. This ties the marrow aspirate relationship to the instrument’s broader clinical role. [Data: Entitie